WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:32.760
Can I start by paraphrasing a quote from our
Chief Executive the other day, who said in

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:38.449
an internal meeting, ‘Amongst all the bureaucratic
things we have to do, it is this sort of stuff,

00:00:38.449 --> 00:00:43.620
dealing with cancer, helping people who are
in difficulty, that puts fuel in our tank,’

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:52.809
and actually I think it is why we are here,
it is why we exist. There is barely a family

00:00:52.809 --> 00:01:00.710
in this country that has not been touched
by cancer, and it brings with it fear, anxiety,

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:06.590
deep grief and, for a number of people, a
deep sense of relief when they are cured.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:12.530
And the cure rate is going up, so that at
the moment 50% of people will expect to be

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:18.140
cured from their cancer. However, the scale
of the problem is pretty significant; there

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:26.409
are about 300,000 new cancers a year, and
of those, about 130,000 people die from it.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:30.539
At any one time, there are about 2 million
people in this country who have cancer, and

00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:36.140
if you think of that as the size of a city,
it is a lot of people.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:44.170
In the five-year forward view, there was a
vision to prevent cancer, to identify it early

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:51.880
and to treat it better than we do at the moment.
Many people point to the fact that our cancer

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:58.939
survival rates are not as good as the European
average, and indeed, that is true. However,

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:06.630
one of the things that the Independent Cancer
Review, chaired by Harpal Kumar, has shown

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:13.170
is that we can exploit some pretty significant
ambition within our Health Service; not just

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.959
to reach European average but to do a hell
of a lot better, and I think that is utterly

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:24.830
achievable.
They have outlined a series of six high-level

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.819
strategic areas that they feel that we as
a system – and by system, I mean the NHS

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:37.590
in general – needs to focus on. The first
is around prevention and public health, because

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:42.980
the evidence suggests that four out of ten
cancers could be prevented by modifying lifestyle,

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:50.650
whether that is related to smoking, obesity
or other modifiable factors.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:57.769
The second area that they are keen for us
to focus on is earlier diagnosis. It is quite

00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:06.650
clear from a lot of research that we are not
diagnosing patients as quickly as we could

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:12.730
in this country compared with some others.
The gains there are significant not just in

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:19.470
terms of human suffering, but they are significant
in terms of money that can be released to

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:26.489
be spent on other things. To give you an example:
if you have colorectal cancer and you divide

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:33.989
it into four degrees of severity, the lower
degrees of severity you would expect nine

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:41.849
out of ten people to survive. If someone presents
at the more severe end of the spectrum, only

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:49.500
one out of ten people will survive, and part
of that pursuit of survival will be costly

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.209
in terms of suffering and anguish, and in
economic terms because people need complex

00:03:55.209 --> 00:04:01.400
surgery, radiotherapy and chemotherapy. So
we need to focus quite significantly on getting

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:14.120
to earlier diagnosis, because a late diagnosis
costs about four times as much as early diagnosis.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.870
They hope that through a national fund and
some new models of care, particularly through

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:26.400
our vanguards, that we might be able to bring
some reality to that earlier diagnosis.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:33.430
They also set us quite a significant ambition,
and I think this is a really good one. First

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:41.289
of all, that 95% of people who present with
symptoms which are suspicious, if you like,

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:50.750
of cancer should have a definitive diagnosis
within four weeks. Frankly, that is not unreasonable,

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:56.669
and frankly, if it were me, I would expect
it and hope for it to be quicker than that,

00:04:56.669 --> 00:05:02.690
and so a subset of that ambition is that they
would want 50% of people to know within a

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:11.270
couple of weeks. Part of that requires us
ensuring that GPs have much quicker access

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:18.990
to certain diagnostic tests, and that brings
with it a number of considerations which we

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:25.240
will have to make as an NHS about the availability
of those tests, not just during the working

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:32.310
week, but at other times, and also ensuring
that we have the capacity to deal with increased

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:38.319
demand and that we have people trained appropriately
to do the tests and to interpret them.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:44.139
The third area that they are keen to focus
on is that they say, ‘Let us make patient

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:46.300
experience in the realm of cancer as important
as clinical effectiveness and safety,’ and

00:05:46.300 --> 00:06:02.270
I think this could extend well beyond cancer
into the way we look at our services in general.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:07.759
More specifically, with respect to cancer,
they think that patients should have much

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:15.819
greater access to online tests, they think
that there should be personal clinical nurse

00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:21.590
specialists available to help people who have
cancer, and that we need to think quite hard

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:26.380
about developing some metrics on working out
whether we are taking patient experience as

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:32.240
seriously as we could.
The fourth area relates to living with and

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:38.440
beyond cancer. So they are keen to see us
develop a set of standards, some clarity over

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:43.919
what we would consider to be a recovery package
for people who have cancer, and for those

00:06:43.919 --> 00:06:49.699
who are not going to recover, to ensure that
we have good end-of-life planning for those

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:57.550
people. I think that is just a matter of compassion
and decency in a National Health Service.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:05.270
One of the issues about the recovery package
is that the majority of people who survive

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:11.960
cancer are over the age of 65, many of them
have a set of other comorbidities which will

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:19.490
require attention, and I think one specific
area that comes with the fear, anxiety and

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:25.199
difficulty of dealing with cancer are some
quite significant mental health challenges,

00:07:25.199 --> 00:07:30.800
which may be temporary or may be permanent,
and we are going to need to give some thought

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:37.949
as to how we focus on that.
The fifth area that the review suggests we

00:07:37.949 --> 00:07:46.319
pay attention to is upgrading our equipment.
Much of the equipment which is used for treating

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:53.599
cancer is expensive, it is technology which
is advancing quite rapidly, and we need to

00:07:53.599 --> 00:08:01.289
have a national plan for how we replace our
capital equipment such as CT scanners, linear

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:06.300
accelerators, and so on and so forth. However,
we do not need to just upgrade our equipment;

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:10.500
we need to upgrade our workforce, and I have
already alluded to that and we need to work

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:16.300
closely with Health Education England on that.
Another area which we have discussed at this

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:21.639
Board which I think provides us with massive
opportunities – and I will allude to that

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:25.800
in a couple of minutes – and another area
we need to upgrade, is our pathology services.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:31.099
Not to suggest in any way that our pathology
services are not up to scratch, but in the

00:08:31.099 --> 00:08:36.800
same way that technology, science and so on
and so forth has influenced the way we deliver

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.470
children’s heart surgery, the advent of
molecular techniques which can probe deeply

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:49.480
into the cell is rapidly changing the way
that we can make diagnostic decisions and

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.330
therapeutic decisions for people with different
types of cancer.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:09:03.170
The final area, which is really very much
within our gift, that the review proposes

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:10.550
is that we should think more about how we
commission generic services for cancer. At

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:15.420
the moment, we have quite a fragmented commissioning
system. We commission for different types

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.260
of cancer, we commission for different types
of radiotherapy, we commission for different

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:26.140
types of chemotherapy. Actually, from a patient’s
point of view they just want to get the treatment,

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:32.510
and actually, from the point of view of a
clinician sitting in a specialist unit trying

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:39.170
to deal with somebody with complex cancer,
they do not want to have to be thinking about

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:44.690
the way we commission the service as deciding
what treatment they offer; they simply want

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:50.760
to be in a position where they can offer the
best possible treatment to that individual.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:58.100
This is about simplification of some of our
bureaucratic processes, frankly.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:10.450
Finally, there is the issue of cost, and there
are two things here. First is: what does the

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:16.880
review think the ‘do nothing’ option is?
They think that is really very expensive.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:24.090
The cost in 2012/2013 of dealing with cancer
was £6.7 billion, and they expect that to

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:36.530
rise to £13 billion a year in the financial
year starting 2020, whereas the implementation

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:42.720
of the recommendations in the review are estimated
to cost somewhere between £700 million and

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:50.710
£1.4 billion a year. Paul may want to say
something about this, but our preliminary

00:10:50.710 --> 00:11:02.060
assessment would agree with that.
Firstly, congratulations to Harpal Kumar and

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:08.500
the review panel. I think they have produced
something which makes good clinical sense,

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:15.880
which has the interests of patients, who are
anxious and frightened, at its heart. They

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:22.610
have proposed 96 recommendations. We are in
the process in NHS England of picking through

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:28.810
those in our internal board, of trying to
ensure that we as an organisation function

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:39.490
in a coherent way and that we work together
to deliver what we need to. The final place

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:43.180
where this will be brought together – and
Simon Stevens may want to say something about

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:51.540
this – is amongst the seven chief executives
of the arm’s-length bodies, because I think

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:59.650
every organisation in the NHS has a part to
play in this improvement ambition.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:07.130
Just one final thing. Whilst this might appear
isolated, there are other things going on.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:12.710
We have discussed at this Board the future
opportunities that genomics can bring, we

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:20.880
have discussed the opportunities of molecular
diagnostics; and actually, as the world’s

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:27.750
biggest semi integrated healthcare system,
and as the healthcare system that is most

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:32.750
advanced of any healthcare system in the world
in tackling the genomics issue, we have a

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:41.810
unique to actually diagnose cancer earlier,
to focus our treatment in a highly specific

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:45.960
way, and to improve the survival rates for
the citizens of this country.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:51.550
Thank you, Bruce. Do you wish to rise to Bruce’s
invitation?

00:12:51.550 --> 00:13:01.250
No. He said it: the six, now the seven, chief
executives of the national leadership bodies

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:06.460
of the NHS are meeting regularly, we are charting
the collective implementation of the forward

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:10.420
view. One of the key priorities there is the
Cancer Taskforce, but what we also want to

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:15.700
see, as well as the national implementation,
is frankly tapping in to the magic ingredient

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:19.100
of this particular process, which is that
it has not just been done internally by the

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:24.660
NHS; it has been co-designed by a much broader
group of people with an interest in cancer,

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:29.310
including patients’ groups, research groups,
the party organisations. We want to make sure

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:34.210
that, given most of those have fantastic representation
and energy locally as well in their local

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:37.960
communities, they are part of the big conversation
about what needs to happen to drive improvement

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:41.900
right across the country. Frankly, because
we think this model is the right one, it is

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:45.140
what we want to reproduce with the Mental
Health Taskforce, which will be producing

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:50.950
its report in September/October; again, with
significant leadership by Mind, Rethink, Young

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:58.940
Minds and various others. Similarly on the
Maternity Taskforce, this is the way we have

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:03.580
to do our business.
Again, very well done; it is a very good piece

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:08.860
of work. I completely agree with Simon; one
of the major reasons it is a very good piece

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:15.590
of work is that it was the whole community
working together, so lots of good things in

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:21.800
it. It is along the pathway, and it is deep
in each bit of the pathway, so fantastic.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:28.050
However, effectively we are switching to implementation
risk now, away from design risk, in that sense,

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:33.000
so I think I would just pick out a few things
from the paper about what needs to happen

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:41.200
for this to be successful. One of them is
data transparency and the sharing of it, because

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:48.330
if this is going to be done in a way which
does not break the bank then that sharing

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:54.210
of knowledge and having interventions in one
part of the pathway which save money and suffering

00:14:54.210 --> 00:15:06.590
in other parts of the pathway has to happen.
How this is implemented needs to be not just

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:15.860
taking it into the NHS England Cancer Programme
Board, but that collective needs to continue.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:21.490
I think there is a risk of cherry-picking,
‘Let us do the ones we like.’ I think

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:24.770
that is wrong for a number of reasons. One
is that some things will get prioritised more

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:28.670
than others, but also I bet the things we
pick will be the things which will be shiny

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:32.910
and new and expensive, and the things which
save money and are hard work and involve working

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:40.110
across boundaries and working in the community
with social care, whatever, would get neglected.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:48.370
But it is really good, so well done.
To build on Ciarán’s point, really, three

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:55.050
things, because clearly this is the clearest
exposition of a vision for cancer treatment

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:59.650
that I have seen; I actually understand it,
it is very clear. There are three things that

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:05.050
we need to keep an eye on, and it reflects
how we see these reports. When we talk about

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:08.750
cancer, I am really interested in the demographics;
so the point that has been made about who

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:17.740
gets cancer, I think we should be aware of
how we track that. It is not just that the

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:21.770
poor get cancer more than the rich; it is
that their experience of the treatment is

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:28.170
different, and within that group, it is very
difficult to ignore issues of race and some

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:36.600
of the stuff that I get from certain ethnic
minority groups. I am interested in how we

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:41.160
track that, because I think it maps onto Ciarán’s
point about the things that we do not talk

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:45.370
about and the things that we tend to assume
are happening but actually are not, that end

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:48.230
up costing us a fortune because they are not
happening.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:52.870
The second point is to support the point you
made about mental health. I am on the Mental

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:56.230
Health Taskforce, and I am just interested
in how we are going to connect up the two.

00:16:56.230 --> 00:17:00.630
It is something that I have raised with the
Chair, but I think it is an underlying point,

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:05.740
and I guess if we do not deal with the emotional
impact then I do not think we are going to

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:11.579
deal very well with the physical impact, so
I am interested in seeing more about that.

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:16.870
The third point is more about – and I have
not memorised the whole cancer report – but

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:22.579
it strikes me that one of the issues about
treatment is geography. So if you are in a

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:31.510
rural area, one of the things that really
impacts is the travel and the sheer grind

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:34.970
of getting to somewhere, as opposed to somewhere
getting to you. I think that is a big issue

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:39.150
for lots of people, so I just wondered, having
seen some really innovative services that

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:48.510
bring the treatment to the individual, whether
the geography issue – bringing the service

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:55.730
to the person – is part of the challenge.
Particularly around chemotherapy, for instance,

00:17:55.730 --> 00:18:00.420
where the travel is as draining as the treatment;
the travel there is as draining as the treatment,

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:07.450
the travel back almost mitigates the treatment.
I am just wondering about those three things,

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:13.400
really: measuring impact demographically;
the connecting with the Mental Health Taskforce

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:20.820
and how we take that forward; and the rural
issues.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:24.440
I have being mulling it over. I think we need
to do some work with the Mental Health Taskforce,

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:31.310
and I think we will take that outside of this
room. Geography is clearly an important issue,

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:36.220
and actually it does not just pertain to cancer,
but I think you are particularly right for

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:42.380
people going for chemotherapy, and we are
focusing on how we get chemotherapy to people

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:51.280
rather than people to chemotherapy.
I would just like to say something about data.

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:55.870
I think data is really important, and I have
to say, Ciarán, I have been really disappointed

00:18:55.870 --> 00:19:03.700
– and this is our fourth Cancer Review – at
how little really granular, easily understandable

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:13.050
data is out in the public domain on cancer.
We have started a discussion on that, and

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:18.150
I would like to reassure you that within a
year, you will be seeing something dramatically

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:25.930
different. For example, I would expect that
we can see by geographical region, by CCG,

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:33.300
in some cases by organisations, different
survival rates for different types of cancer.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:49.190
I am meeting all sorts of barriers on that,
but they will be broken down.

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:55.340
We already can see there are huge differences
in the staging of cancers across the country;

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:59.860
it is only about between two thirds and three
quarters that actually get staged. There is

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:06.530
a huge difference in the one-year survival
rates by CCG, linked in part to diagnosis

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:12.280
and to some of the economics, their race,
other points of connections that the Health

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:17.740
Service has. That is in the CCG delivery dashboard,
but tracking one-year survival rates, and

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:23.830
as a result the early diagnosis performance
across the country, is obviously absolutely

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:29.700
crucial. Then there are some things around
the vast amounts we are spending on chemotherapy.

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:37.570
We do not have e-chemo systems in every hospital,
we are not recording on the systematic therapy

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:42.310
database all of the use of the spending there;
we just have to get very hard nosed about

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:47.470
that. We are doing it through the Cancer Drugs
Fund, through the spec com commissioning.

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:50.430
We need all of this because if we do not,
we are not able to capture any of the data

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.810
about what is actually working in practice
and what the differential performance is.

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:58.110
All of those tools are at our disposal. This
is not waiting for some kind of big new technical

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:01.880
solution; this is just about us choosing to
get serious – us, the NHS collectively – on

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:09.700
these issues.
I will give you an example. 50% of our cancers

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:16.690
are due to four cancers. It has been a real
struggle to find out what the one-year survival

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:28.500
for those is at any level of granularity,
and we will solve that very quickly.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:33.720
This is allied to Victor’s point, but it
is not to do with tracking; it is really to

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:39.610
do with investment, and I think it is a question
of linking your strategic priorities three

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:46.200
and five, really. Strategic priority five
very clearly sets out the areas in which you

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:57.030
would like to see greater investment, and
three is focusing on improving the experience.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:05.140
My point is really about the psychosocial
support and what that does in aiding recovery,

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:13.820
and in particular with the people Victor has
highlighted, the people who tend to fall outside

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:22.810
of the social network and do not have the
greatest level of support very easily. Ideally,

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:28.650
rather than just focusing on the workforce
deficits where one is talking about acute

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:36.640
support, also talking about where there is
a need for psychological and social support.

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:44.520
So an investment there appears to be something
that we could usefully add in to that fifth

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:54.460
strategic priority.
I think the Board would wish to join us in

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:59.860
commending the report and expressing our gratitude
to Harpal Kumar and all of the others who

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:05.640
served on the taskforce. It is an impressive
list, it is a very inclusive list. It sets

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:10.920
us a number of quite demanding things to do,
but I think what it has helped us to do is

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:14.890
understand what the priorities are and how
we should address them. We particularly take

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:20.320
note of the data issue that has been raised
this morning, and we will take it forward.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:26.760
I think, Bruce, we would anticipate quite
regular reporting back to the Board on next

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:31.690
steps, progress and implementation, as we
shall do with mental health and other taskforces.

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:37.010
This is a way to identify key priorities for
not just NHS England, but the NHS more generally.

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:39.300
Thank you very much.

